    <statement><p>Find a vector parametric equation <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> for the line 
through the points <m>P = \left(-5,4,-1\right)</m> and <m>Q = \left(-6,-1,1\right)</m>
for each of the given conditions on the parameter <m>t</m>.</p>
<p>(a) If <m>\vec{r}(0) = \left\lt -5,4,-1\right&gt;</m> and 
<m>\vec{r}(2) = \left\lt -6,-1,1\right&gt;</m>, then</p>
<p><m>\vec{r}(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>(b) If <m>\vec{r}(5) = P</m> and 
<m>\vec{r}(7) = Q</m>, then</p>
<p><m>\vec{r}(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>(c) If the points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> correspond to 
the parameter values <m>t = 0</m> and 
<m>t = -2</m>, respectively, then</p>
<p><m>\vec{r}(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(-5,4,-1\right)+\frac{t}{2}\mathopen{}\left&lt;-1,-5,2\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(-5,4,-1\right)+\frac{t-5}{2}\mathopen{}\left&lt;-1,-5,2\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(-5,4,-1\right)+\left(-\frac{t}{2}\right)\mathopen{}\left&lt;-1,-5,2\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>SOLUTION</p>
<p>The line has direction <m>\vec{PQ} = \left\lt -1,-5,2\right&gt;</m></p>
<p>(a) Using the point <m>P=\left(-5,4,-1\right)</m> as a base point, the vector equation of the line is
<me>\vec{r}(t) = \left(-5,4,-1\right)+\frac{t}{2}\mathopen{}\left\lt -1,-5,2\right&gt;</me>
(b) The vector equation of the line is 
<me>\vec{r}(t) = \left(-5,4,-1\right)+\frac{t-5}{2}\mathopen{}\left\lt -1,-5,2\right&gt;</me>
(c) The vector equation of the line is 
<me>\vec{r}(t) = \left(-5,4,-1\right)+\left(-\frac{t}{2}\right)\mathopen{}\left\lt -1,-5,2\right&gt;</me></p>
</solution>
